<h2>2. AD8232 ECG Electrode Placement & Skin Preparation</h2>
<p>The AD8232 instrumentation amplifier measures microvolt cardiac biopotentials across the chest or limbs using a 3-lead differential configuration:</p>

<table>
  <tr>
    <th>Electrode Lead</th>
    <th>Wire Color</th>
    <th>Anatomical Placement</th>
    <th>Physiological Function</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>RA (Right Arm)</strong></td>
    <td>Red</td>
    <td>Right inner forearm / wrist, or right upper chest below right clavicle</td>
    <td>Inverting input of differential instrumentation amplifier</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>LA (Left Arm)</strong></td>
    <td>Yellow</td>
    <td>Left inner forearm / wrist, or left upper chest below left clavicle</td>
    <td>Non-inverting input of differential instrumentation amplifier</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>RL (Right Leg)</strong></td>
    <td>Green / Black</td>
    <td>Right lower abdomen, right hip, or right ankle</td>
    <td>Driven Right Leg (RLD) active common-mode rejection reference</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>Skin Preparation Instructions for Low-Noise ECG:</strong>
  <ol>
    <li>Clean skin sites with an alcohol swab to remove epidermal oils and dead stratum corneum cells.</li>
    <li>Allow the skin to dry completely before applying gel electrodes.</li>
    <li>Ensure lead cables hang relaxed without mechanical tension to prevent baseline wander caused by cable movement.</li>
  </ol>
</div>

<h2>3. Pulse Transit Time (PTT) Blood Pressure Principles</h2>
<h3>3.1 Vascular Biomechanics (Moens-Korteweg & Hughes Equations)</h3>
<p>Pulse Transit Time (PTT) is the time required for the arterial blood pressure wave to travel from the aortic valve to a peripheral vascular bed (such as the thumb). When the left ventricle contracts, it depolarizes (manifested as the steep <strong>R-peak</strong> on the ECG) and forces open the aortic valve. The resulting hydrostatic pressure wave propagates along the elastic arterial wall at the Pulse Wave Velocity (PWV):</p>

<p align="center"><em>PWV = &radic;( (E &middot; h) / (&rho; &middot; d) )</em></p>

<p>Where <em>E</em> is Young's elastic modulus of the arterial wall, <em>h</em> is wall thickness, <em>d</em> is vessel diameter, and <em>&rho;</em> is blood density. As arterial blood pressure (<em>P</em>) rises, the arterial wall stretches, increasing its effective stiffness according to Hughes' empirical exponential law:</p>

<p align="center"><em>E = E<sub>0</sub> &middot; exp(&gamma; &middot; P)</em></p>

<p>Consequently, higher blood pressure accelerates wave propagation, yielding a <strong>shorter Pulse Transit Time (PTT)</strong>. Conversely, lower blood pressure relaxes arterial tone, yielding a <strong>longer PTT</strong>:</p>

<p align="center"><em>SBP = a &middot; ln(PTT) + b</em><br>
<em>DBP = c &middot; ln(PTT) + d</em></p>

<h3>3.2 Baseline Calibration Constants</h3>
<p>For an average adult arterial pathway length of approximately 0.8 meters (heart to hand), the calibrated resting coefficients implemented in <code>codes/main.py</code> are:</p>
<ul>
  <li><em>a = -72.5</em>, &nbsp; <em>b = 518.0</em> (Systolic Blood Pressure, SBP)</li>
  <li><em>c = -48.0</em>, &nbsp; <em>d = 343.0</em> (Diastolic Blood Pressure, DBP)</li>
</ul>

<table>
  <tr>
    <th>Pulse Transit Time (PTT)</th>
    <th>Systolic Pressure (SBP)</th>
    <th>Diastolic Pressure (DBP)</th>
    <th>Physiological Interpretation</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>205 ms</strong></td>
    <td>131 mmHg</td>
    <td>87 mmHg</td>
    <td>Elevated vascular tone / vasoconstriction / stress</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>250 ms (Normal)</strong></td>
    <td>118 mmHg</td>
    <td>78 mmHg</td>
    <td>Optimal normotensive resting baseline</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>295 ms</strong></td>
    <td>106 mmHg</td>
    <td>70 mmHg</td>
    <td>Vasodilation / deep physical relaxation</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2>4. Step-by-Step Calibration Against an External Cuff</h2>
<p>To calibrate the PTT model to your specific arterial length and vascular compliance:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Sit quietly in a comfortable chair for 5 minutes. Attach the 3 ECG electrodes (RA, LA, RL) and place your thumb gently on the MAX30102 sensor.</li>
  <li>Run <code>main.py</code> in Thonny or on standalone battery power. Observe the average resting PTT displayed on the LCD (e.g., <code>248 ms</code>).</li>
  <li>Simultaneously take a measurement with your external digital arm or wrist cuff (e.g., <code>120 / 80 mmHg</code>).</li>
  <li>If your cuff measures 120 / 80 mmHg at 248 ms, calculate the exact offsets:
    <pre>
import math
ptt = 248.0
log_ptt = math.log(ptt)  # ~5.513
b = 120.0 - (-72.5 * log_ptt)  # b = 120 + 399.7 = 519.7
d = 80.0 - (-48.0 * log_ptt)   # d = 80 + 264.6 = 344.6
    </pre>
  </li>
  <li>Update lines 181-184 in <code>codes/main.py</code> with your custom <code>cal_b</code> and <code>cal_d</code> values for personalized precision!</li>
</ol>

<h2>5. LCD 1602 Real-Time User Interface</h2>
<p>The LCD 1602 provides instant visual feedback across two 16-character rows:</p>

<pre>
+----------------+
|HR: 60  SpO2:99%&hearts;|   &lt;-- Row 0: Heart Rate + SpO2 + Animated Flashing Heart
|BP:118/78  248ms|   &lt;-- Row 1: Systolic/Diastolic BP (mmHg) + Measured PTT (ms)
+----------------+
</pre>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Col 15 Heart Icon (&hearts;):</strong> Pulses solid black for 200 ms with every detected capillary beat.</li>
  <li><strong>Leads Off Indicator:</strong> If ECG electrodes are detached, Row 1 automatically displays <code>BP: CONNECT ECG </code> while HR and SpO2 continue running smoothly from the MAX30102.</li>
  <li><strong>Thumb Off Indicator:</strong> When thumb is removed, the display reads <code>PLACE THUMB PAD </code> within 1.5 seconds.</li>
</ul>

<h2>6. Master Firmware Deployment (main.py)</h2>
<p>To establish autonomous operation without a computer connected:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Open <strong>Thonny IDE</strong> and connect to the ESP32-CAM.</li>
  <li>Open <code>codes/main.py</code> from the project directory.</li>
  <li>Select <strong>File &rarr; Save As... &rarr; MicroPython device</strong> and name it exactly <code>main.py</code>.</li>
  <li>Unplug the USB cable from your PC and power the ESP32-CAM from any standard 5V USB wall adapter, power bank, or Li-ion battery pack.</li>
  <li>The monitor will initialize within 2 seconds, displaying <code>ECG+PPG+BP READY</code> and prompting you to place your thumb.</li>
</ol>

